This is all NH3.1.3.  I had ascended the twelve classes when this list 
had only thirty entries, so I tended to think of having an ascension 
rate of 1 in 2.5, averaged over all classes.  However, this was a bit 
pretentious, since the 8 games before should be counted in; but at least 
I had this rate during the first 30 games on that computer :-).  Anyway, 
NH3.1.3 was a much simpler game than NH3.4.3; you did not need 
reflection once you had passed level 18 and unicorn horns worked all the 
time.  Demogorgon could be killed with a wand of death, the riders were 
just pathetic.  (Nonetheless, Famine managed to burn an amulet of live 
saving of one of my characters.)

Also at the time I was pretty motivated, because in NH3.1.3, it was an 
achievable goal of ascending all characters, and I tried to do so in 
expending the least no. of characters (and of course, I did not reroll 
any character to obtain better stats; once a character has started, it 
is my child I have to care for; so quitting would mean abandoning it). 
With NH3.4.3, I don't feel a comparable motivation, since there are just 
too many combinations of classes to do all of them, and I don't have 
enough time.  (But I think some slash'em classes are challenges, that I 
would like to meet; essentially ice and flame mages, lycanthropes, 
vampires and undead slayers.)

The two worst experiences in the list given above were the deaths number 
18 and 19. Rambler was my sixth tourist and I was almost sure he would 
make it, having lost only one character with a comparable AC before.  He 
had quaffed from a fountain in the hope of getting a wish (I think he 
still lacked magic resistance), and had obtained a water demon instead. 
  He stupidly fought that demon who summoned Yeenoghu.  Exit Rambler. 
This was the first time I appreciated that Yeenoghu is strong...

The death of Gawain was even worse.  He had so much respected his code 
of chivalry...  There was a barracks and in order to clean it out, he 
woke up all the soldiers with a whistle.  So he killed them all in the 
same space.  Then he decided to polypile their armor a little.  An iron 
golem turned up.  Now, I had dealt with iron golems before and never 
found them a problem.  But I realized after the demise of Gawain that I 
had not assessed their strength correctly, because I only had met them 
at much later stages of the game, where I was much better equipped. 
Gawain had an AC of -11 and 80 HP.  He also had three blessed scrolls of 
scare monster and Excalibur.  I thought that I did not want to use up 
the scare monster scrolls so fast and would drop one of them only, if I 
could not kill the iron golem fast enough.  Well, the golem needed just 
two turns to kill Gawain...  This was the stupidest of my deaths ever :-(.

